    {
      "file": "server/api.js",
      "line": 57,
      "endLine": 101,
      "pattern": "function buildPrompt({ contextPath, focusPath, summary, question, scope, prior }) {",
      "description": "The prompt is where product behaviour gets defined. It does not inline the images. It tells Claude to open the PNG files with its Read tool, marking one as FOCUS and the other as context only. It then lists earlier answers (with \"do NOT repeat\"), the structured summary and any typed question. Next come rules for working out intent, keeping answers short and avoiding markdown, and a strict `<answer>`/`<diagram>` output contract that the next step parses.",
      "vaayu": {
        "annotations": [
          {
            "id": "imgs",
            "line": 58,
            "pattern": "const images = focusPath",
            "label": "Focus vs context images"
          },
          {
            "id": "fmt",
            "line": 94,
            "pattern": "Output format (exactly):",
            "label": "Output contract"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "server/api.js",
      "line": 124,
      "endLine": 154,
      "pattern": "function runClaude(prompt, addDir, onChild) {",
      "description": "`runClaude` starts the Claude Code CLI headless: `-p --output-format json --dangerously-skip-permissions --add-dir <askDir>`, run in `CLAUDE_CWD`. Point that at a repo and Claude can read your code while it reviews. `--add-dir` gives it access to the snapshot folder. The process is bounded by `CLAUDE_TIMEOUT_MS`, and the JSON envelope is checked for `is_error` before the `result` string is accepted."
    },
    {
      "file": "server/api.js",
      "line": 103,
      "endLine": 122,
      "pattern": "function parseAnswer(text) {",
      "description": "`parseAnswer` enforces the contract from `buildPrompt`. It pulls out `<answer>` and the optional `<diagram>`, strips stray ```mermaid fences, and removes leftover markdown (`#`, `**`, backticks) that would show up literally in an Excalidraw text element. If the model skipped the tags, the whole reply becomes the answer, so the user always gets something."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/claude.js",
      "line": 398,
      "endLine": 402,
      "pattern": "const b = boundsOf(block);",
      "description": "Back in the browser. The answer is built with `cardElements` as a header, a violet background and wrapped text. If there is a diagram, it is converted from Mermaid to editable Excalidraw shapes and stacked below. Every element is tagged with `customData.claude` and `askedAt`. Then the placeholder is removed and `findFreeSpot` runs again for the real size, and the card is inserted as one undoable step. Those tags close the loop: `newInk` uses `askedAt` to decide what counts as new next time, and `priorAnswers` reads the card text back as conversation history.",
      "vaayu": {
        "title": "Closing the loop",
        "takeaway": "Answer cards are ordinary board elements that also store the conversation's state.",
        "annotations": [
          {
            "id": "meta",
            "line": 368,
            "pattern": "const meta = { question: question || \"\", scope: t.scope, askId: data.id, sessionId: data.sessionId, askedAt };",
            "label": "Card metadata incl. askedAt"
          },
          {
